LATEST IMPLEMENTATION — MB-03D
compare_branches_v2<'a>(
    expected: ManifestIdentityV2,
    current: &'a ChainV2,
    candidate: &'a ChainV2,
) -> Result<BranchComparisonV2<'a>, ForkChoiceErrorV2>

Both inputs must already be complete accepted ChainV2 values. Check the independent
expected identity in this order: current configuration, current genesis, candidate
configuration, candidate genesis. Errors WrongConfiguration { branch } and
WrongGenesis { branch } use BranchSideV2::{Current, Candidate}. The comparison
accepts no raw block, peer-advertised height, caller-supplied score, cache or
storage-generation parameter.

Each accepted block uses the same configured literal PoW target. The normalized
post-genesis score equals height, from 0 through 4,095, preserving the ordering of
constant-target accumulated work. The shared genesis contribution cancels:
total work = (height + 1) * W, where W is the same positive constant for each block.
The implementation only compares heights. It performs no work multiplication or
accumulation, and therefore cannot overflow those operations. The score is not a
claim about the number of hashes actually attempted. A lower individual block hash,
attempt count, nonce, money, population, approval count, encoded byte count, resource
counter, timestamp or local generation adds no score. A configuration or genesis
that differs from the independently selected identity is rejected.

ForkChoiceV2::{KeepCurrent, PreferCandidate}: a strictly higher candidate height
wins; every tie keeps the ordered current input. This is a sticky local preference,
not a globally unique tie winner or human finality. Two peers with different
current tips of equal score may remain different. The chain cap or a terminal
timestamp can prevent further extension. Future variable-target or human-weighted
profiles need separately reviewed replacement rules.

BranchRelationV2 distinguishes Identical, CandidateExtendsCurrent,
CurrentExtendsCandidate and Diverged. Compare actual accepted block hashes from
genesis until the first mismatch; the last matching block is the common ancestor.
Matching accounts or state roots alone do not define ancestry. The result returns
the ancestor's complete checkpoint: configuration, genesis, height, hash and
ledger root.

BranchComparisonV2 has private fields and no public constructor or decoder. It
borrows both inputs immutably; safe Rust prevents either input from changing while
the result remains in use. Its accessors are identity(), relation(), choice(),
common_ancestor(), current_score(), candidate_score(), current_checkpoint(),
candidate_checkpoint() and selected_chain().

selected_chain() returns &'a ChainV2 pointing to the complete preferred input.
Manifest, blocks, accounts, membership, approvals, permanent records, population
and resource counters remain together. No state copying or grafting, mutation,
I/O, reexecution or publication occurs. Copied diagnostic enums and checkpoints
can become stale after the borrow ends. A future durable API must recheck the
exact current and candidate histories under its own lock.

Comparison uses at most 4,096 accepted hash pairs, or 8,192 fixed-header hashes,
with O(1) extra state and no heap allocation. Each branch retains its separate
caps: 4,096 blocks including genesis, 16 MiB of MRB2 bytes, and 1,048,576 cumulative
abstract state/configuration items. Comparison does not relax or combine those
caps. Hashing uses the existing stack preimage; actual hash magnitudes never rank
branches. No encoding, hash domain, codec, dependency, fixture, work target or
monetary rule was introduced or changed.

